# bubblewrap -- the sandbox itself. bwrap is the program flatpak execs to
# construct every application's namespaces, mounts and seccomp filter.
#
# PACKAGED SEPARATELY RATHER THAN VENDORED, AND THAT IS THE DECISION IN THIS
# RECIPE. flatpak ships bubblewrap as a meson subproject and will build its own
# copy unless pointed at a system one (-Dsystem_bubblewrap=). Taking the
# subproject would work and would be less effort. It is refused because a
# vendored binary is invisible to tape's dependency graph and to the signed
# index -- and this is the component that ENFORCES THE SANDBOX. A sandbox helper
# that nothing can audit, update or even enumerate is the worst possible thing
# to hide inside another package's build.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/containers/bubblewrap/releases/download/v0.11.0/bubblewrap-0.11.0.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=988fd6b232dafa04b8b8198723efeaccdb3c6aa9c1c7936219d5791a8b7a8646
SRC_DIR=bubblewrap-0.11.0

# NOT INSTALLED SETUID, AND THAT IS A PROPERTY OF THE KERNEL RATHER THAN OF THIS
# RECIPE. bwrap needs either unprivileged user namespaces or the setuid bit.
# pkgs/linux/config/common.config sets CONFIG_USER_NS=y, so an ordinary user can
# create the namespaces bwrap needs and no setuid binary has to exist. That is
# strictly the better outcome: a setuid helper is attack surface that runs as
# root before it drops privileges, and the whole reason it exists is to avoid
# needing root.
#
# -Drequire_userns=false is upstream's default and is left alone deliberately.
# It only takes effect when bwrap IS installed setuid, which it is not here, so
# changing it would be a no-op that looks like a hardening decision.
#
# THE TWO COMPLETION SCRIPTS ARE PAYLOAD AND BOTH DEFAULT TO ENABLED:
#   -Dbash_completion=disabled  bash IS packaged, but bash-completion is not, so
#                               the script would install into a directory nothing
#                               sources -- payload with no reader.
#   -Dzsh_completion=disabled   there is no zsh in this distribution at all.
# Both are the installed_tests shape: files carried to every system, decided by a
# default nobody looked at, with a build that succeeds either way.
#
# -Dtests=false. UPSTREAM DEFAULT IS TRUE; build-time only.
#
# -Dselinux=disabled. At 'auto' it resolves to off because no SELinux is
# packaged -- the right outcome for the wrong reason. Named so it stays that way
# if libselinux ever appears for some other consumer.
#
# -Dman=disabled. At 'auto' it would try, and the man pages need tooling that is
# not packaged.
MESON_ARGS="-Dbash_completion=disabled -Dzsh_completion=disabled \
-Dtests=false -Dselinux=disabled -Dman=disabled"
